WEBVTT

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.220
Intelligence must be packaged for the audience and the decision.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.320
A long technical report may be useful to an analyst but ineffective for an executive.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.855
A short executive summary may clarify risk but be too vague for detection engineering.

00:00:11.855 --> 00:00:16.340
Useful CTI products include short reports, briefings, tickets, detection stories,

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:20.210
watchlists, blocklists, executive summaries, incident response notes,

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.450
vulnerability prioritization memos, and threat hunting hypotheses.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.300
The format should match the workflow.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.560
Operational teams often need intelligence in task-shaped formats.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.340
A detection engineer may need a behavior description, expected telemetry, example fields,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.760
ATT&CK mapping, false-positive notes, and test guidance.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.800
A SOC analyst may need triage steps, enrichment context, affected assets,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.200
and escalation criteria.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.280
A vulnerability manager may need evidence of exploitation, affected products,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.065
exposure context, compensating controls, and a recommended priority.

00:00:50.065 --> 00:00:53.265
Packaging is part of the intelligence work, not an afterthought.

00:00:53.265 --> 00:00:55.465
Good packaging also makes limits visible.

00:00:55.465 --> 00:00:58.640
If a watchlist includes indicators, it should explain how old they are,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.200
what source they came from, whether they are recommended for blocking or enrichment,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.335
and what false positives are plausible.

00:01:04.335 --> 00:01:07.610
If an executive summary describes a threat trend, it should explain confidence,

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.760
business relevance, and what decision is needed.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.494
A product that hides uncertainty can look polished while making action riskier.

00:01:13.494 --> 00:01:16.408
STIX and TAXII are common concepts in structured sharing.

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:18.810
STIX, the Structured Threat Information Expression,

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.328
provides a way to represent threat intelligence objects and relationships.

00:01:22.328 --> 00:01:25.660
TAXII, Trusted Automated Exchange of Intelligence Information,

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.550
supports exchanging that structured information.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.105
These standards can help organizations share and automate parts of CTI,

00:01:31.105 --> 00:01:32.690
but they do not replace analysis.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:37.185
A well-formatted object can still be stale, irrelevant, or low confidence.

00:01:37.185 --> 00:01:38.130
Handling rules matter.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.770
The Traffic Light Protocol, or TLP,

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:43.150
is used to communicate how shared information may be redistributed.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.235
At a practical level, TLP:CLEAR can be shared broadly,

00:01:46.235 --> 00:01:48.470
TLP:GREEN is limited to the community,

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.480
TLP:AMBER is limited to those who need it to protect their organization,

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.640
and TLP:RED is restricted to named recipients or a very limited group.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.365
Teams must follow the version and policy used by their sharing community.

00:01:59.365 --> 00:02:03.580
Speed should not override accuracy, sensitivity, privacy, or source restrictions.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.745
During an incident, it can be tempting to forward everything quickly.

00:02:06.745 --> 00:02:09.910
That can expose sensitive investigations, violate sharing terms,

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.990
or spread unverified claims.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.841
Good CTI sharing is timely and disciplined.

00:02:13.841 --> 00:02:17.320
It states confidence, marks handling requirements, protects sensitive details,

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.485
and gives the recipient enough context to act responsibly.

00:02:20.485 --> 00:02:23.885
The best format is usually the format that fits an existing workflow.

00:02:23.885 --> 00:02:26.165
If engineers work from tickets, create a clear ticket.

00:02:26.165 --> 00:02:29.220
If executives make decisions from short briefs, write a brief.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.130
If detections are managed as stories with acceptance criteria, use that structure.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.150
CTI gains power when it moves through channels people already use.
